    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-343354",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules",
      "problem": "Given that $y=2x{e}^{6x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=($\n{{field-1}} $){e}^{6x}$",
      "worked_solution": "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=2x$ and $v(x)={e}^{6x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=6{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2)({e}^{6x})+(2x)(6{e}^{6x}) \\\\  & =(12x+2){e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
